
1. Login to GitHub and create a new repository.

2. Create a Python file named hello.py.

3. Write a simple Python program in the file.

4. Commit the changes to the GitHub repository.

5. Copy the GitHub repository URL.

6. Login to Slack and create a new workspace.

7. Create a new Slack channel for Jenkins notifications.

8. Open Jenkins in the browser and login.

9. Click on New Item.

10. Enter the project name and select Freestyle Project.

11. Click OK to create the project.

12. Go to Source Code Management (SCM).

13. Select Git and paste the GitHub repository URL.

14. Enter the branch name as */main or */master.

15. Configure GitHub credentials if the repository is private.

16. Go to the Build Steps section.

17. Click Add Build Step and select Execute Shell.

18. Enter the following command for Python:

    python3 hello.py

19. For Java files, use:

    javac Main.java
    java Main

20. Click Apply and Save.

21. Go to Manage Jenkins -> Plugins.

22. Search for Slack Notification plugin.

23. Install the Slack Notification plugin.

24. Go to Manage Jenkins -> System.

25. Find the Slack configuration section.

26. Configure Slack workspace details and credentials/token.

27. Enter the default Slack notification channel.

28. Click Apply and Save.

29. Open the Jenkins project configuration.

30. Go to Post-build Actions.

31. Select Slack Notifications.

32. Enable notifications for successful and failed builds.

33. Click Apply and Save.

34. Open the Jenkins Dashboard.

35. Select the created project.

36. Click Build Now to execute the project.

37. Click Console Output to verify build execution.

38. Open the Slack workspace and check the notification channel.

39. Verify both successful and failed build notifications.


RESULT:

Successfully integrated GitHub with Jenkins for automated
source code retrieval and build execution, and configured
Slack to receive build success and failure notifications.


